  summary: "Return True for a Float that is neither NaN nor positive or negative infinity. Negative numbers and zero are finite too; positivity, integrality and the Int range need separate checks.",
  taskTitle: "Exclude NaN and infinities from measurements",
  purpose: "After parsing text or performing a calculation, check whether the result is a finite number suitable for a measurement. isFinite takes one Float and returns the Bool value True or False. It excludes only NaN and both infinities without changing the value or its sign.",
  setup: "import * as floats names std/float. In fn finiteLabel, value: Float declares a numeric argument that can contain a fraction, and -> String declares the returned text type. fn declares a function. floats.isFinite value passes its one argument after a space. if returns the then text finite for True, or the else text non-finite for False.",
  after: [
    "12.5, -3.0, -0.0 and 1e100 all produce finite. 1e100 is ten to the power 100, a large but finite Float. -0.0 is negative zero and passes this check just like positive zero. Finiteness therefore does not establish that a measurement is positive.",
    "floats.nan () returns NaN, a value indicating that a calculation did not produce a valid number. positiveInfinity and negativeInfinity produce the two infinities. The () passed to these functions is a Unit value, used when no ordinary input data is needed. All three final lines produce non-finite.",
    "pub effect fn main is the entry point. pub makes it available externally, and effect marks work such as displaying output. do runs println in order, printing each string from finiteLabel on its own line. Parentheses pass an inner function’s result as one argument to the outer function."
