  comparisonBefore: "TypeScript Number.isFinite(value) performs the corresponding check without converting the value. It returns true for finite numbers and false for NaN or infinities. The examples turn those booleans into the same finite/non-finite display strings. The final export {} makes this example an independent module without changing the calculation.",
  comparisonAfter: "Both print the same strings for the same seven numeric inputs. The expressions are similarly concise for this check. Seseragi requires a Float argument by its type. In TypeScript, use Number.isFinite for its non-converting behavior, distinguishing it from the global isFinite function that also converts inputs such as strings.",
  details: [
    "Positive and negative finite values, including both signed zeroes, produce True. NaN, positive infinity and negative infinity produce False. The result is a Bool directly, not an exception or a missing value. No repair or rounding takes place.",
    "True does not imply that the value fits Int. 12.5 has a fractional part, and 1e100 is larger than the maximum Int, 9007199254740991. Use std/int parse to read an integer count; when converting a Float to an integer, decide rounding and check range separately.",
    "Float parse successfully reads explicit NaN, Infinity and -Infinity spellings. An input field that forbids those values should use isFinite after parsing succeeds. parse checks string syntax; isFinite classifies an already-existing Float.",
    "It is also a mistake to equate finite with exact decimal arithmetic. Float uses a 64-bit binary representation, so a rounded approximation can still be finite. Finiteness, an acceptable application range, and sufficient precision are separate conditions."
